Question: 15. What is Intrinsic Lock (Monitor)?

Input:
Two synchronized blocks using the same object.

Code:
private final Object lock = new Object();

public void update() {
    synchronized (lock) {
        // critical section
    }
}

Output:
Only one thread can own lock's intrinsic monitor at a time.

Explanation:
Every Java object can act as an intrinsic monitor. Entering synchronized(lock) 
requires ownership of that object's monitor. Exiting the block releases it, including when an exception propagates.

Important:
Synchronization coordinates threads only when they use the same monitor. 
Locking two different objects does not protect one shared invariant.

