Question: 31. What are Virtual Threads?

Input:
One blocking request-handling task.

Code:
Thread v = Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> handleRequest());
v.join();

Output:
The request runs on a virtual thread.

Explanation:
Virtual threads are java.lang.Thread instances scheduled by the Java runtime rather 
than permanently tied one-to-one to an OS thread. They are designed for high-throughput applications
with many concurrent tasks that spend substantial time blocked, especially on I/O.

They improve scalability, not the speed of an individual task. They are not 
intended to make long-running CPU-intensive work faster. Avoid pooling virtual threads; use one virtual thread per task.

